Testing IoT Device Security (Default Passwords & Open Ports)

Aim
To analyze security weaknesses in a simulated IoT device by identifying:
Open ports
Running services
Service version disclosure
Default/weak authentication
Unencrypted communication

Procedure

Step 1: Deploy Vulnerable IoT Simulation
docker run -d -p 8090:3000 --name juiceshop bkimminich/juice-shop
The application was accessed at:
http://localhost:8090

Step 2: Identify Host IP Address
ipconfig in cmd in normal not kali
The host IP (e.g., 192.168.0.148) was noted for scanning.

Step 3: Perform Network Scan from Kali Linux
nmap localhost
nmap -sV 192.168.0.148
This scan performed:
Port scanning
Service detection
Version detection
Banner grabbing

Step 4: Service Version Detection
The -sV flag enabled identification of service versions and fingerprints.
This helps attackers identify known vulnerabilities.

Step 5: Access IoT Dashboard
Open browser and navigate to:
http://localhost:8090
The Juice Shop interface represents an IoT management panel.

Step 6: Test Default/Weak Credentials
Login functionality was tested using administrator credentials.
Successful login indicated weak/default authentication.
admin / admin
admin / password
root / root
user / 1234

Step 7: Analyze Network Traffic
Open Developer Tools → Network Tab
Observed:
HTTP requests
Plain-text data transmission
No encryption (no HTTPS)

Observations
Port 8090 was open (HTTP service active).
Additional Windows ports exposed:
135 (RPC)
139 (NetBIOS)
445 (SMB)
Service fingerprint identified as Juice Shop.
Version information was visible.
MAC address detected during scan.
Communication occurred over HTTP (no SSL/TLS).

Browser analysis showed:
Data transmitted in plain text
No encryption mechanisms present
